(b) whether Government has taken note of persistent enforcement weaknesses despite repeated concerns raised;
(c) the manner in which these enforcement gaps are linked to erosion of trust among consumers in domestic food products and damage to the credibility of genuine farmers producing safe staples; and
(d) the specific measures proposed to strengthen enforcement, such as increased manpower, harsher penalties, rapid testing infrastructure, better traceability to restore agricultural integrity and public confidence?

(a) whether Government recognises that rising climate concerns and rapid urbanisation demand urgent measures to shift people from private vehicles to reliable and affordable public transport;
(b) whether it is a fact that lack of fare and information integration across metro, bus and other transport systems is discouraging commuters from using public transport;
(c) the reasons for continued dependence on informal and unregulated transport modes due to gaps in formal public transport systems; and
(d) the concrete and time-bound steps taken to implement fare integration, strengthen public transport infrastructure and ensure affordable, accessible and sustainable mobility for citizens?

(a) whether persistent concerns exist among rice farmers and mill owners in various parts of the country due to export restrictions on non-basmati in the past causing surplus, warehouse congestion, storage shortages, depressed demand and income losses;
(b) status of rice export policy as of 2026, including APEDA registration norms for non-basmati, its effects on domestic prices, farmer incomes and mill operations in surplus regions;
(c) whether Punjab’s Basmati sector continues to face distress from payment delays, ?2,000+ crore stranded consignments and Iran market uncertainties; and
(d) steps proposed—including diplomacy, storage aid, incentives and market diversification—to resolve these issues and protect affected livelihoods?

(a) whether Government is aware that allocation for agricultural research and education has declined in real terms despite rising climate risks, input costs and low farm incomes;
(b) whether the allocation of ?1.32 lakh crore to the Ministry, when agriculture supports nearly 46 percent of the workforce, reflects adequate priority, given that most funds are absorbed by subsidies rather than productivity investments; and
(c) the reasons as to why expenditure on agricultural education and research has been kept at about ?9,967 crore despite claims of promoting technology led farming and whether continued underinvestment threatens long-term food security and farmer livelihoods?

(a) whether the Labour Codes, allow layoffs of up to 300 workers without prior approval, restrict strike rights with mandatory 14-day notice and mandate 51 per cent union membership for sole bargaining, thereby diluting job security, collective bargaining and post-independence worker protections;
(b) if so, the steps taken to address nationwide protests including the February 12, 2026 Bharat Bandh demanding rollback and concerns over gig/contract workers' inadequate social security; and
(c) the measures to resolve ambiguities in wage definitions, State-level enforcement variations, SME compliance burdens and stagnant Ministry's budget allocation amid rising unemployment and inequality?

(a) whether Government has reviewed compliance of Colleges and Universities in Maharashtra with norms prescribed by the University Grants Commission, particularly regarding faculty strength, infrastructure and teaching quality;
(b) the number of sanctioned teaching posts lying vacant, extent of reliance on ad-hoc or contractual teachers and deficiencies in laboratory and digital infrastructure during the last five years;
